"At Trinity College, Cambridge, he studied mathematics and then philosophy. In 1900 he met the Italian logician Giuseppe Peano at a congress in Paris and saw that the new symbolic logic was the tool he needed. Within a year he had drafted most of The Principles of Mathematics, which argued that the whole of mathematics follows from a small number of logical principles. In the spring of 1901 he found the contradiction now called Russells paradox, and the next decade went to repairing the damage. He and his former teacher Alfred North Whitehead wrote Principia Mathematica, three volumes of symbols published between 1910 and 1913. The publisher expected a loss, and the authors paid part of the cost. Russell said that his intellect never quite recovered from the strain.": "b59ca27a418d",
"He applied the same procedure everywhere. When a way of speaking seems to commit us to strange entities, such as numbers, classes, or the average taxpayer, we should try to restate what is said in terms of things we have better reason to believe in. His slogan was that wherever possible, logical constructions are to be substituted for inferred entities. His logicism was this method applied to mathematics: numbers were to be constructed out of classes, and classes out of logic. The program did not fully succeed, as the lesson on numbers explains. The method survived and became the working practice of analytic philosophy, the tradition in which most English-speaking philosophers still write.": "282d849543f6",
"He lived for another forty-nine years. He supported himself by writing books for the general reader on marriage, education, happiness, power, and the history of philosophy, and with his second wife he ran an experimental school. He married four times. In 1940 a New York court canceled his appointment at City College on the ground that he was morally unfit to teach. Ten years later he received the Nobel Prize in Literature. In 1955 he and Einstein issued a manifesto against nuclear weapons, signed by Einstein a few days before his death. In 1961, at eighty-nine, Russell was sent to Brixton again, for a week, after a sit-down protest in London. He died of influenza in Wales in 1970.": "2e9d022370d5",
"He was taught at home and was often lonely. At eleven his brother began to teach him Euclid, and he later called it one of the great events of his life. He had not imagined that there was anything so delicious in the world. He was also disappointed, because the axioms had to be accepted without proof, and his brother told him that if he did not accept them they could not go on. The wish to find out whether anything could be known for certain, which he traced to that lesson, governed the first half of his working life.": "3bae78fbc470",
"In the same years he changed the way philosophy was done in English. His paper On Denoting of 1905 showed that the grammar of a sentence can hide its logical form, and that puzzles which had seemed deep could be dissolved by rewriting the sentence. From 1911 he taught Ludwig Wittgenstein, whose criticism of his work was so severe that Russell abandoned a book he was writing. He told a friend that he could not hope ever again to do fundamental work in philosophy.": "a59b06dbd74c",
"Russell arrived in China in October 1920 and stayed until the following summer, lecturing in Peking on mathematical logic, the analysis of mind and matter, and social reconstruction. His interpreter was the young linguist Yuen Ren Chao. The May Fourth generation had invited him as a representative of science and reason, and some of his hosts were disappointed to find that he admired much of what they were trying to leave behind. He liked the humor, the courtesy, and what he called the pacific temper of the people he met, and he compared the Wests energy unfavorably with it.": "9b7ea411e294",
"Russell was a critic of religion all his life, and he did not make an exception for Buddhism. In the preface he wrote in 1957 for Why I Am Not a Christian he listed it among the great religions that he thought both untrue and harmful. He objected to belief held without evidence, wherever it was found. He nevertheless regarded Buddhism, especially in its early form, as the least harmful of them, because it had persecuted least. There are points of real contact. His rule that it is undesirable to believe a proposition when there is no ground for supposing it true stands close to the Buddhas advice to the Kālāmas not to accept a teaching on the strength of tradition, scripture, or the standing of the teacher.": "33ba3c8d68ab",
"Russell was born in 1872 into one of the great Whig families of England. His mother and sister died when he was two and his father before he was four. His parents had been freethinkers and had named John Stuart Mill as his godfather in a secular sense, and they left instructions that the children be brought up by tutors who shared their views. The courts set the will aside. Bertrand and his brother Frank were raised at Pembroke Lodge in Richmond Park by their grandmother, a devout and strong-willed woman, in a household of elderly people and hushed voices. His grandfather, the former prime minister, died when the boy was six.": "5d3f68ce7368",
"Russells lasting contribution is a method. Consider the sentence The present King of France is bald. France has no king. Is the sentence true or false? If false, it seems that The present King of France is not bald should be true, and it is not. Some philosophers had concluded that the King of France must have a kind of being, short of existence, so that the sentence could be about him. Russell showed that the puzzle came from mistaking the grammar. The sentence looks like Socrates is bald, a name followed by a description. In its logical form it is three claims joined together: there is a king of France, there is only one, and whoever is king of France is bald. The first claim is false, so the whole is false, and nothing shadowy is needed.": "8bae96fb5591",
"Russells paradox": "a99a9a289aa6",
"Russells reply was characteristically blunt. He said of the later Wittgensteins school that it seemed concerned only with the different ways in which silly people can say silly things. Philosophy, he thought, should be continuous with science and should try to find out what the world is like. A method that has dissolved some confusions has earned its place even if it does not answer everything. On ethics he conceded the ground. He held that judgments of value cannot be proved, said that he found this conclusion hard to live with, and went on campaigning anyway. He never claimed that his politics followed from his logic.": "4ad14d6c31eb",
"The First World War turned him into a campaigner. He opposed British entry into the war and worked for the conscientious objectors. In 1916 he was fined, and Trinity dismissed him from his lectureship. In 1918 he was sentenced to six months in Brixton Prison for an article, and he used the time to write his Introduction to Mathematical Philosophy. In 1920 he went to Russia, met Lenin, and came back an opponent of the Bolsheviks at a time when most of his friends on the left admired them. He then spent most of a year lecturing in Peking, where he fell so ill with pneumonia that a Japanese newspaper reported his death.": "b23a036504c5",
"The Problem of China, published in 1922, is the result. Its central question is whether China can learn Western science and industry without also learning Western militarism and greed. Russell hoped it could, and feared that foreign pressure would leave no choice. The book has the limits of a visitors book. He did not read Chinese, he met mostly intellectuals, and his praise of Chinese patience sometimes slides into the picturesque. It is still worth reading for its refusal to assume that the direction of teaching runs one way. When I went to China, I went to teach, he wrote, but every day that I stayed I thought less of what I had to teach them and more of what I had to learn from them.": "ce90a8b18bef",
"The closest contact is in his account of the self. In The Analysis of Mind, written from the lectures he gave in London and Peking, Russell argued that there is no subject standing behind our thoughts and having them. I think is a misleading piece of grammar, and it would be better to say it thinks in me, or simply that there are thoughts. A person is a series of events connected by memory and causation. He reached this by the method described above, treating the self as a logical construction and not an inferred entity. An early Buddhist would recognize the analysis into aggregates. The difference lies in what follows from it. For Russell it was a result in the theory of knowledge. In the Buddhist path seeing it clearly is meant to loosen grasping, and so to change how a person lives (Dharma and Western Philosophy D2).": "7320a53d8ec2",
"The objection came from his own pupil. Wittgenstein held that Russells method treated ordinary language as a faulty version of logic, when it is in order as it is. To translate The King of France is bald into three logical clauses is to replace one use of words with another, and not to reveal what the speaker meant all along. P. F. Strawson made the point in 1950: a person who utters that sentence today has failed to refer to anyone, and what he says is neither true nor false. On this view analysis explains the puzzles away by changing the subject, and a philosophy that reduces every question to logical form will have nothing to say about ethics, religion, or art, which is where Russells critics thought the deepest questions lay.": "e75c84126379",
